1
00:00:00,046 --> 00:00:02,160
Hi guys, this is Rashid and welcome back.

2
00:00:02,320 --> 00:00:11,840
Today is one of the topics that some of my viewers have been asking to do how to control the robot over the internet, or in another term, the robot teleoperation.

3
00:00:12,014 --> 00:00:22,948
That means your robot is placing somewhere and you are in somewhere else, and you want to control the robot passing all the network and you expect to have the video streaming and the data displaying.

4
00:00:23,278 --> 00:00:26,960
I have made a robot teleoperation before, like two years ago.

5
00:00:27,200 --> 00:00:44,400
At that time I was using a ROS2 middleware called CENOR, which is working time to time, but from my experience and the real use case from customer, I found that the most simplest and most reliable and less complicated is to use browser-based WebRTC.

6
00:00:44,590 --> 00:00:48,612
If you want to know why and how I do it, so let's get started.

7
00:00:48,974 --> 00:01:03,280
As a traditional old school custom UDP or WebSocket communication, you will need to launch a server machine somewhere, and your robot and your web console GUI will need to talk with the server first in order to send or receive data.

8
00:01:03,440 --> 00:01:09,120
And yes, this method also works, but the problem is high latency or it's too slow.

9
00:01:09,390 --> 00:01:13,520
And how about the Cinol ROS2 middleware that I mentioned in the first place?

10
00:01:13,680 --> 00:01:19,360
And yes, it's one of the good technology that you can use and send ROS2 message over the internet.

11
00:01:19,886 --> 00:01:21,920
If you haven't tried, please check it out.

12
00:01:22,126 --> 00:01:26,880
And there is some demo that's showing you can have a video and a control on the robot.

13
00:01:27,118 --> 00:01:34,800
I've been using CNO for a while, but there are some requests that keep coming like the video is quite laggy, can you make it much faster?

14
00:01:35,022 --> 00:01:39,520
Can I open the camera from my phone or iPad to display my face on the robot?

15
00:01:39,694 --> 00:01:43,360
Can I talk with people near the robot or hear sound from the robot side?

16
00:01:43,662 --> 00:01:49,124
An end-user computer is not Linux, just only Windows, they want something easy to use.

17
00:01:49,870 --> 00:01:53,412
Then I think the better solution is to use WebRTC.

18
00:01:53,966 --> 00:02:01,188
With the same system, you have robot at the field and your computer in Office Network and Signaling Server on the cloud.

19
00:02:01,486 --> 00:02:13,444
At first, the robot will send offer of SDP and ICE to signaling server, and once your computer accesses the web console, it will send answer of SDP and ICE to signaling server as well.

20
00:02:14,126 --> 00:02:17,440
Those SDP and ICE will be exchanged for each peer.

21
00:02:17,582 --> 00:02:23,360
Then after established, the peer will see each other, and then there is no need for signaling server anymore.

22
00:02:23,630 --> 00:02:35,920
If you would like to learn more about WebRTC, I pretty recommend on these videos how does WebRTC work, Crash Course from Dennis Ivey, and WebRTC Crash Course from Hussain Nasser.

23
00:02:36,160 --> 00:02:38,372
They have done a pretty good job for explaining.

24
00:02:38,894 --> 00:02:41,700
So, why browser-based WebRTC?

25
00:02:42,094 --> 00:02:46,560
Somebody will say, hey, there is also Python AIORTC.

26
00:02:46,766 --> 00:02:50,800
Yes, of course, AIORTC Python package is another option.

27
00:02:51,118 --> 00:02:54,852
It can create WebRTC peer and handle the WebRTC session.

28
00:02:55,054 --> 00:03:07,200
You can run this on the robot side as one peer, but again, if your web console side can only run web browser, you will need to implement an original HTML JavaScript WebRTC anyway.

29
00:03:07,406 --> 00:03:22,480
So instead of you going to head 8 on both Python WebRTC from AIORTC and JavaScript WebRTC from Google, you just need to know only JavaScript WebRTC because it could run on both robot side and web console side.

30
00:03:22,640 --> 00:03:32,800
To conclude, why I prefer to use browser-based WebRTC, because first, it could run on mostly any computer, any OS which has a web browser.

31
00:03:32,960 --> 00:03:38,000
You don't need to worry about dependencies or install extra packages, just the web browser.

32
00:03:38,160 --> 00:03:43,972
Second, it's easy to expand, as said earlier, not even just control the robot remotely.

33
00:03:44,270 --> 00:03:51,712
If you need to stream your video and talk with others near a robot, Implementing those things on JavaScript is not a hassle at all.

34
00:03:51,918 --> 00:04:00,592
Third, if you don't know your robot public IP or is behind that, or you don't know what is DDS, QOS, etc.

35
00:04:01,198 --> 00:04:06,672
So with WebRTC, you don't need to care much about that because it's designed to handle it for you.

36
00:04:06,832 --> 00:04:13,152
For the browser-based WebRTC video is optimized and it has adaptive bitrate control.

37
00:04:13,518 --> 00:04:22,912
For example, if your network is not good, so instead of keep displaying 4K video, it will become low value solution and allow you to see the video in real time.

38
00:04:23,118 --> 00:04:24,992
So your latency is quite stable.

39
00:04:25,152 --> 00:04:31,712
Also for data channel, it's implemented based on UDP, so data packet will arrive right on time.

40
00:04:31,872 --> 00:04:42,432
And lastly, you could get rid of expose ROS message to the outside world, just keep ROS in local network and let WebRTC handle for global network communication.

41
00:04:42,734 --> 00:04:51,472
Before going too far, I would like to remind you that this browser-based WebRTC method is just one of the idea that I think is good for my application.

42
00:04:51,822 --> 00:04:55,312
There is no right or wrong or which one is better than which one.

43
00:04:55,598 --> 00:04:59,588
It just depends on your background experiences that which one is best for you.

44
00:05:00,270 --> 00:05:03,072
And this video is sponsored by PCB Way.

45
00:05:03,502 --> 00:05:16,004
PCB Way provides you a service not only making a PCB, they also specialize on CNC machining from simple to complicated design and 3D printing from variety of filaments you can choose.

46
00:05:16,462 --> 00:05:31,908
For example, flexible material or TPU or transparent material, which I think is really cool for some products, or many tough materials better than PLA, which could be used for high temperature, outdoor or daily usage product.

47
00:05:32,558 --> 00:05:39,140
Also, one of my favorite is the laser cutting on carbon fiber plate, which is good for robot prototyping.

48
00:05:39,630 --> 00:05:43,364
It's for a lightweight but still strong and just a simple design.

49
00:05:44,014 --> 00:05:53,348
I've been using PCB Wave for a couple of times when I need a rapid prototype of the PCB or a hardware manufacturing like this carbon fiber plate.

50
00:05:54,030 --> 00:05:58,272
So don't let your project just in mind or just a file in the computer.

51
00:05:58,574 --> 00:06:02,148
You can check on PCB services and try it out for your next project.

52
00:06:02,766 --> 00:06:04,272
So it's too much of talking.

53
00:06:04,432 --> 00:06:07,428
Let me show you the WebRTC template that I have made.

54
00:06:08,174 --> 00:06:11,428
This is a GitHub repository for WebRTC template.

55
00:06:11,854 --> 00:06:13,860
You can have a look from this URL link.

56
00:06:14,158 --> 00:06:16,100
It's public and free to download.

57
00:06:16,430 --> 00:06:19,364
So the file structures are shown in this picture.

58
00:06:19,758 --> 00:06:35,952
So we have some part that related directly to WebRTC starts from console.html, answer.js, mainanswer.js, offer.js, main offer.js, signalingserver.js, and offer.html.

59
00:06:36,112 --> 00:06:46,032
And lastly, there are Python scripts that has to be running on the robot, json ros2 converter.py, local data server.py, ws python client.py.

60
00:06:46,192 --> 00:06:52,112
The main role of this file is to convert and exchange data from WebRTC to ROS message.

61
00:06:52,334 --> 00:06:55,300
Here is the overview where the code should be running.

62
00:06:55,598 --> 00:07:04,132
So on the robot side, there is ROS2 node running, for example, like your laser scanner, motor driver, reading sensor data, and so on.

63
00:07:04,814 --> 00:07:15,872
And another three Python scripts from this WebRTC template folder, and the robot will be running web browser from offer.html and offer.js and main offer.js will be calling.

64
00:07:16,032 --> 00:07:25,712
On the cloud or server side, signaling server.js has to be running at first, and this server is hosting a web console as console.html.

65
00:07:25,966 --> 00:07:31,492
Then on user PC, they can use any web browser to access the cloud server domain name.

66
00:07:31,886 --> 00:07:36,868
Then they could see the web console on the screen and able to control the robot from this computer.

67
00:07:37,326 --> 00:07:41,348
Then I'm going to show you what's going to happen to make this thing work.

68
00:07:41,870 --> 00:07:50,724
First, on the robot side, the local data server, the built Python client and JSON ROS2 converter will be running and also your ROS2 node.

69
00:07:51,312 --> 00:07:59,232
Here, if we look on the code of local dataserver.py, first make sure to install WebSockets with 10.4 version.

70
00:07:59,342 --> 00:08:02,628
The local WebSocket port is on 8888.

71
00:08:03,246 --> 00:08:07,940
And if we look down here, we will start a WebSocket server as localhost.

72
00:08:08,686 --> 00:08:11,744
With the echo function, and this will be running forever.

73
00:08:11,982 --> 00:08:18,244
Inside echo function, there will be to the build sjs and the buzz Python, which are two clients.

74
00:08:18,798 --> 00:08:23,424
So once each client is connected, the server can know it from the ending path.

75
00:08:23,584 --> 00:08:31,556
The concept here is simple whatever message from Python client receive it will send to JS client and vice versa.

76
00:08:31,950 --> 00:08:35,236
Meaning each client will receive a message from another client.

77
00:08:35,822 --> 00:08:38,304
That's it for the local data server WebSocket.

78
00:08:38,574 --> 00:08:41,184
Next, what does the WS Python client do?

79
00:08:41,344 --> 00:08:47,824
Is if we check on the code again, first make sure to install WebSocket client version 1.4.2.

80
00:08:48,174 --> 00:08:51,844
On top here, we define the UDP socket for receive and send.

81
00:08:52,366 --> 00:08:57,380
These sockets are used between WS Python client and JSROS2 converter.

82
00:08:57,902 --> 00:09:04,740
And this is a WebSocket URL client ending with Python client, so the WebSocket server could recognize this.

83
00:09:04,944 --> 00:09:08,356
And down here, there is send thread as global valuable.

84
00:09:08,654 --> 00:09:15,824
We're going to start WebSocket app with these events for on message, on open, on error and on close.

85
00:09:16,270 --> 00:09:18,544
We have sender worker as another thread.

86
00:09:18,704 --> 00:09:24,464
On open event, once the web socket is open, then we start the sender thread after that.

87
00:09:24,624 --> 00:09:34,624
And on message, whatever message it received from JS client is going to dump by pickle, then send via UDP socket to JSONROS2 converter.

88
00:09:34,784 --> 00:09:42,340
Inside the sender worker thread, it will be a while loop and keep listening on UDP socket from JSON ROS2 converter.

89
00:09:42,734 --> 00:09:49,504
Then whatever message it received, it will be dumped as JSON, then pass it to WebSocket.js client.

90
00:09:49,806 --> 00:09:56,740
The JSON ROS2 converter will be using UDP socket to send and receive message from the BUS Python client.

91
00:09:57,166 --> 00:10:00,612
So on the code, you could see the send receive ports are opposite.

92
00:10:01,294 --> 00:10:05,988
The send packets object here is what we're going to send to the BUS Python client.

93
00:10:06,382 --> 00:10:11,664
We need to make sure that these object keys are meshing when we read it on WebRTC.

94
00:10:11,824 --> 00:10:14,144
Then we can make a class for ROS2 node.

95
00:10:14,304 --> 00:10:23,024
These publishers are the input from user web console like number, PDM command slider, or some button and command velocity.

96
00:10:23,184 --> 00:10:29,344
On the subscribers, this node is listening for a ROS topic like robot status, sensor data.

97
00:10:29,582 --> 00:10:33,156
In this case, it's just float in string and boolean data.

98
00:10:33,614 --> 00:10:36,804
Then this data will be showing on the web console screen.

99
00:10:37,262 --> 00:10:43,184
So in the ROS callback, we will put the ROS topic data to the send packets object in each key.

100
00:10:43,344 --> 00:10:52,452
Inside timer loop, we keep listening from UDP receive socket from the BUS Python client, load by pickle once, then load again by JSON.

101
00:10:52,814 --> 00:11:01,264
Inside the pass data, we will check the key like timestamp, input number, pdvm command, button state, or command velocity.

102
00:11:01,486 --> 00:11:04,704
For timestamp, we just print it out and don't do anything.

103
00:11:05,102 --> 00:11:12,864
For other keys, we're going to put the pass data to each ROS publisher so the other ROS node could see this input command.

104
00:11:13,038 --> 00:11:20,260
Then lastly, we are sending out the send packets back to the BUS Python client and to the WebRTC further.

105
00:11:20,782 --> 00:11:24,132
Those are three Python scripts running on the robot side.

106
00:11:24,526 --> 00:11:32,304
For signaling server, these comment outlines are for HTTPS and lower block is just for normal HTTP.

107
00:11:32,942 --> 00:11:36,384
So you can comment out either of the above block or this block.

108
00:11:36,544 --> 00:11:47,460
On the connection even, if the client connects as offer client, then we put it as db US offer, or if the client is answer client, then it put as the BUS answer.

109
00:11:48,110 --> 00:11:54,212
For answer client, if offer come first, then it will send a receive offer back to answer.

110
00:11:54,606 --> 00:11:59,376
Or if answer client came first, then just set that flag as answer came first.

111
00:11:59,854 --> 00:12:07,396
On message even, if the message is from the offer client, then we check that it's a ping message or offer message.

112
00:12:07,982 --> 00:12:13,380
And in case the answer comes first is set, then we send the offer back to answer immediately.

113
00:12:14,062 --> 00:12:21,412
But if the message is from answer client and offer is already here, then we send answer back to the offer client.

114
00:12:22,094 --> 00:12:29,124
On close event, if the offer client is closed then just set it as now and close the web socket.

115
00:12:29,710 --> 00:12:33,696
Similar if the answer client is closed then just set it as now.

116
00:12:34,190 --> 00:12:42,976
But if the offer client hasn't closed yet, it will send restart offer back to offer client to let it know that the answer client is gone now.

117
00:12:43,214 --> 00:12:47,812
And there is a loop just to check the in case of the offer is not closed properly.

118
00:12:48,206 --> 00:12:54,020
The signaling server will not receive anything from the offer client, then it will close all the web sockets.

119
00:12:54,336 --> 00:13:00,096
Next, once the offer peer is started, it will start WebSocket to connect with signaling server.

120
00:13:00,270 --> 00:13:03,376
So it will start by open up offer.html.

121
00:13:03,536 --> 00:13:10,816
This page is very simple, just import offer and main offer.js scripts and has only a video box showing.

122
00:13:10,976 --> 00:13:12,496
Here is offer.js.

123
00:13:12,816 --> 00:13:15,856
We have defined WebRTC variables on top here.

124
00:13:16,110 --> 00:13:19,056
Next, we create WebSocket as offer client.

125
00:13:19,534 --> 00:13:23,300
So we need to specify the URL of signaling server.

126
00:13:23,822 --> 00:13:29,188
After that, it will also start a WebSocket local client as the BOS local.

127
00:13:29,614 --> 00:13:33,136
This is to communicate with the local data server.py.

128
00:13:33,422 --> 00:13:36,772
So we need to uncomment this to allow data communication.

129
00:13:37,294 --> 00:13:49,444
You could see on message event, whatever it received from the BOS local WebSocket, it will use WebRTC data channel to send it further, meaning the data will be passed through to the web console.

130
00:13:50,158 --> 00:13:57,616
And because offer page is running on the robot side, we need to initialize the media device like camera or microphone.

131
00:13:57,776 --> 00:14:02,116
On the code, media device in it will check on the available devices.

132
00:14:02,606 --> 00:14:14,256
So in case if I would like to use a specific camera, for example, with the name as fake device, then I will check the label of each device and return the correct device ID.

133
00:14:14,542 --> 00:14:18,736
After got device ID of camera, then put it as constraint object.

134
00:14:18,896 --> 00:14:25,936
If no microphone, then put audio as false, and we can use getUserMedia with the constraint to open the camera.

135
00:14:26,096 --> 00:14:28,016
In the end, we call create offer.

136
00:14:28,336 --> 00:14:36,016
After that, create offer will be using an SDP data, for example, what media device will be used in WebRTC.

137
00:14:36,654 --> 00:14:43,936
Inside the function, it will create peer connection and create its own offer, then send to signaling server.

138
00:14:44,270 --> 00:14:51,812
For create peer connection function, it will create RTC peer connection by using provided stunt turn server.

139
00:14:52,366 --> 00:14:56,260
We can use the free one from Google or we can use our own.

140
00:14:56,686 --> 00:14:58,416
So just specify it here.

141
00:14:58,894 --> 00:15:01,220
For local test, we don't need to specify it.

142
00:15:01,710 --> 00:15:11,456
Then if there is a limode video, we will put it on the local video and we're going to get track from local stream and add track to the peer connection.

143
00:15:11,950 --> 00:15:13,796
Then data channel is created.

144
00:15:14,318 --> 00:15:21,376
And if there is message coming from WebRTC Data Channel, we just pass through it to the WS local web socket.

145
00:15:21,696 --> 00:15:32,816
And at the end, the ICE candidate, which is the optimized path for WebRTC connection, could be updated, and we would like to send the latest ICE to the signaling server.

146
00:15:33,102 --> 00:15:39,716
On the diagram here, you could see both of SDP and updated ICE candidate sent to the signaling server.

147
00:15:40,718 --> 00:15:48,292
So once Web Console or answer peer is started, it will connect with Signaling Server by WebSocket as AnswerClient.

148
00:15:48,974 --> 00:15:53,828
On the code here, we need to make sure the URL of Signaling Server is correct.

149
00:15:54,478 --> 00:16:01,124
On message event, if the message is alive, that is supposed to be an offer from offer client.

150
00:16:01,582 --> 00:16:08,932
Then if created answer is false, we are going to run start connect with a bit delay of 3 seconds.

151
00:16:09,806 --> 00:16:11,984
Next step is to create answer.

152
00:16:12,590 --> 00:16:15,140
The sequence is pretty much same as offer.

153
00:16:15,630 --> 00:16:23,428
So start create peer connection and make its own SDP and Ice, then send it back to signaling server.

154
00:16:24,270 --> 00:16:30,084
On the diagram here, offer will receive answer, then it will add answer to peer connection.

155
00:16:30,574 --> 00:16:37,668
Inside create peer connection of answer, just start RTC peer connection with server, same as before.

156
00:16:38,126 --> 00:16:42,692
Then now the remote stream we have here is the robot video streaming.

157
00:16:43,246 --> 00:16:49,984
We're going to put the stream to the robot video source object, which is the DOM element on the web console.

158
00:16:50,190 --> 00:16:55,204
On DataChannel, we set the flag as receive data web RTC as true.

159
00:16:55,886 --> 00:17:02,628
Then we know that the message coming is JSON object, so we can pass it and get data from each key.

160
00:17:03,118 --> 00:17:10,788
So need to make sure that the key are passing is same as the key of send packets from JSON ROS2 converter here.

161
00:17:11,534 --> 00:17:17,424
Once the WebRTC connection is established, this will be a through peer-to-peer connection.

162
00:17:17,678 --> 00:17:27,556
Input command from Web Console will be sent from answer to offer and pass through local web socket inside the robot and construct as ROS topic.

163
00:17:27,824 --> 00:17:40,144
Vice versa, the robot's data or sensor data will be sent from local web socket to offer, then pass through answer via WebRTC data channel and display on screen.

164
00:17:40,304 --> 00:17:43,172
Next, let's do some tests on the local machine.

165
00:17:43,566 --> 00:17:49,188
First, we start signaling server, then local data server and the BS Python client.

166
00:17:49,710 --> 00:17:55,204
Then on for terminal, we source ROS environment and run JSON ROS2 converter.

167
00:17:55,854 --> 00:17:58,340
Then open up offer.html.

168
00:17:58,990 --> 00:18:05,104
You could see a dummy video box showing and signaling server is getting ping message from offer.

169
00:18:05,838 --> 00:18:10,564
If check on the browser console, you will see SDP offer printing out.

170
00:18:11,758 --> 00:18:20,164
Then on the new tab, we open Web Console on the answer peer, Wait a couple seconds until the WebRTC is established.

171
00:18:20,622 --> 00:18:23,396
Then you could see the video streaming on web console.

172
00:18:24,462 --> 00:18:30,116
On the fifth terminal, we can run ROS to topic echo on web number topic.

173
00:18:30,542 --> 00:18:37,028
Then on web console, we can change the number and press enter to send this back to ROS environment.

174
00:18:37,934 --> 00:18:45,188
If we echo on the web button topic, then you could see the state keep toggle when pressing on green button.

175
00:18:46,254 --> 00:18:55,812
If we echo on the web PWM command topic and slide on the slider, the value will be changing so we can use to control the servo motors.

176
00:18:57,806 --> 00:19:02,276
And for nipple joystick, we can echo on the command velocity topic.

177
00:19:02,734 --> 00:19:08,868
So the value of linear X and Angular set will be changed according to joystick position.

178
00:19:09,230 --> 00:19:11,460
We can use this to teleopt the robot.

179
00:19:12,590 --> 00:19:18,084
For robot status, in this case we have float integer, string, and boolean message.

180
00:19:18,702 --> 00:19:24,304
So we can publish this from ROS one by one, then it will be showing on the web console.

181
00:19:24,814 --> 00:19:26,788
It's like a robot telemetry data.

182
00:19:29,518 --> 00:19:37,284
I have a small rover set up here, and I'm going to control this wheel model by using this ATC Mob ROS board.

183
00:19:37,710 --> 00:19:43,460
If you don't know what is it you can check more detail about this board from the link in the description.

184
00:19:44,174 --> 00:19:59,236
We have a small micro servo connect on the board and the Wi-Fi router and the Raspberry Pi 5 as a robot computer and USB web camera and DC DC regulator and everything is powered from this single battery.

185
00:20:00,046 --> 00:20:03,652
On robot first terminal, I run Micro ROS agent.

186
00:20:04,174 --> 00:20:09,636
Then second terminal is CMO app UROS utils with Well Odom converter.

187
00:20:10,350 --> 00:20:12,644
Third terminal is signaling server.

188
00:20:13,326 --> 00:20:15,908
Fourth terminal is local data server.

189
00:20:16,302 --> 00:20:19,364
And fifth terminal is the BUS Python client.

190
00:20:20,238 --> 00:20:24,048
Then on JSON ROS2 converter, I need to modify something.

191
00:20:24,208 --> 00:20:31,460
So I add a publisher to the CMO app server command so we can control the server from the web console.

192
00:20:32,398 --> 00:20:35,108
